From 583489ff69ef6456339f93d11166a1ad02729031 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 06:58:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10084/11362] Record the open exp6605 receipt defect that
 blocks a nine-task cascade

exp6605 blocks on `gpu_process_receipts` while every precondition passes. It is the
root of a cascade: exp6608 blocks with `upstream_blocked` and `missing_rows: 216`, and
nine further tasks fail their gate check only with "upstream artifact not found".

Half of it is fixed. The receipt carried a session from a llama-server killed by the
operator reboot; a reboot-killed session can never report a clean shutdown, and the
checkpoint re-attached it on every retry. Discarding the checkpoint cleared that.

Half is open, and this entry exists so the remaining half is not re-derived. With one
clean session over all 216 rows, exactly one of the nineteen authenticity criteria
fails: `worker_pid_present` is not True on every `during` sample. Sampling is not the
cause -- 30 during samples against a >= 2 requirement. Something briefly made the model
process invisible to a GPU sample mid-run.

Written down rather than left in a session, because the finding cost a full diagnosis
pass and the next reader would otherwise start from "integrity did not complete", which
names nothing.

Includes the instruction not to fix it by relaxing the predicate. It is a fabrication
gate, and "ignore samples that did not see the worker" is the shape an attacker wants.

